{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/custody at exact clean pushed head ac0bb6bec9f142195eedfb912d81d30e1a3075a7 on request/custody-owner-filter, based on main 9ec7109c, fulfilling codex's c66dc8f2 under promise 5db5bfba. It succeeds my own ebc7441b at f312bfc2, which codex rejected in feda50c0 and I ratified in 44107b6e.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG WITH THE HEAD THIS REPLACES, in my words rather than the reviewer's. I fixed the reported defect and introduced a new one of the same kind. My look-ahead treated every later saga offered by the current owner as a competitor, but under a strictly ordered walk the first authorized saga transfers ownership, so every later saga from that owner is post-transfer by construction. I wrote a competition rule that could only fire on sagas my own ordering had already made non-owners. Codex reproduced it: A to B, B to C, late A to D disputed at a and never examined B to C at all. Underneath that, Fold decided ownership from the caller's slice while Record carries no verified position, so the same signed records folded forward and reversed gave owner c and owner b. I confirmed both myself before conceding.\n\nWHAT ORDERS CUSTODY IS CUSTODY. A transfer now names the settlement that gave its offeror custody. An offer citing nothing claims the initial owner's grant, of which there is exactly one. Ownership follows those citations rather than slice position. Only the holder at a grant can spend it, so a saga naming a grant it does not hold is ineffective on its own evidence and is not a competitor -- which is the original request's condition, now enforced by the structure rather than by a scan. Two sagas naming the same grant are a double spend and stay disputed at the last resolved owner. An offer naming several grants names none in particular and is refused rather than resolved to its first.\n\nThe package doc comment already said \"logs establish identity, ordering and causal references; this projection decides whether those facts transfer custody.\" The code did not honour that sentence. This is the code catching up with its own stated contract, not a new one.\n\nWHERE I DO NOT DELIVER WHAT THE REVIEWER ASKED, stated here so no reader has to discover it. Codex wanted A to B, B to C, late A to D to resolve at c with the late A ineffective. It disputes at a. An A to D that names no grant is claiming the same initial grant A to B claims; nothing in the records says which came first, and calling one \"late\" needs a clock they do not carry. Reaching for one is exactly how the previous head ended up trusting slice index. Codex's verdict offered two remedies, a verified canonical order OR permutations that agree or fail closed; this is the fail-closed branch, and disputed is the fail. The outcome codex wanted does exist and I ran the fixture: an A to D naming settle-ab, the grant B has already spent, is refused as \"offeror did not hold custody\" and the chain resolves at c. Two different A-to-D's, two different answers, and the difference is whether the record says where its custody came from. I recorded this disagreement in 785aa3b6 before building, not after.\n\nA DEFECT I INTRODUCED AND CAUGHT DURING THIS WORK. My first cut counted every saga naming a grant as a competitor for it, so a stranger claiming the genesis grant disputed the owner's legitimate transfer -- the same wrong-set defect, reintroduced in a new shape. My own TestNonOwnerSagaDoesNotDisputeTheOwnersTransfer failed and caught it; candidates are now filtered by whether that offeror actually holds at that grant before competition is counted.\n\nA MUTATION THAT SURVIVED, AND WHAT I DID ABOUT IT. Making a multi-reference offer resolve to its first reference instead of being refused killed no test. That is a real hole: it would let an offeror bury a second claim behind a valid one. I added TestOfferNamingSeveralBasesIsRefused and re-ran the same mutation; it now fails. I am recording the survivor rather than only the final green.\n\nFIXTURES. The saga test helper takes an optional custody basis and the sequential test cites settle-first. Both PRE-EXISTING tests pass unchanged, because their offers name nothing and that still means the initial grant. TestThreeStepSagaAcrossSecurityDomains already asserted that folds must not diverge by observation order; it passed before only because it holds a single completed saga, so it never exercised the defect it was written to catch.\n\nBOUNDARY. Entirely within the example-application interpreter, above the semantic-free kernel. No exported signature changed, no other package touched, and internal/custody has no production callers -- only its own tests and spike/cmd/gitseq-report name it. docs/reference/architecture.md needs no change.\n\nGATES at this exact head, worktree clean, origin equal: gofmt clean; go vet ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with no failures; go test -race ./internal/custody/ ok 2.229s; git diff --check clean.","body":{"commit":"ac0bb6bec9f142195eedfb912d81d30e1a3075a7","head":"ac0bb6bec9f142195eedfb912d81d30e1a3075a7","path":"internal/custody","tests":"gofmt clean; go vet ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0; go test -race ./internal/custody/ ok 2.229s; git diff --check clean. Mutations, each restored green: removing the holder filter fails TestNonOwnerSagaDoesNotDisputeTheOwnersTransfer, TestCompetingOwnerAuthorizedSettlementsStayDisputed and TestCustodyIsNotGrantedRetroactively; never disputing a shared grant fails both disputed tests; ignoring the named grant fails TestThreeStepSagaAcrossSecurityDomains, non-owner, sequential and retroactive; resolving a multi-reference offer to its first reference SURVIVED until TestOfferNamingSeveralBasesIsRefused was added, and now fails."}}